      <!-- ===== CARDS ===== -->
      <div id="human-environment-intro" class="topic-card"><div class="sec-head"><span class="sec-num">1</span><h2>Human–Environment Interaction — मानव-पर्यावरण अंतःक्रिया</h2></div><p><strong>Human–Environment Interaction</strong> humans और environment के बीच <strong>mutual relationship</strong> है — humans environment को <strong>modify</strong> करते हैं और environment humans को <strong>influence</strong> करता है।</p><div class="formula-box">Human–Environment Interaction = Humans Modify + Environment Influences</div><p><strong>Key Aspects:</strong></p><ul class="bullet-list"><li><strong>Humans depend on environment:</strong> Air, water, food, resources</li><li><strong>Humans modify environment:</strong> Deforestation, pollution, urbanization</li><li><strong>Environment affects humans:</strong> Climate, disasters, resource availability</li></ul></div>

      <div id="anthropogenic-activities" class="topic-card"><div class="sec-head"><span class="sec-num">2</span><h2>Anthropogenic Activities — मानवजनित गतिविधियाँ</h2></div><p><strong>Anthropogenic Activities</strong> humans द्वारा की गई वे activities हैं जो environment को <strong>impact</strong> करती हैं।</p><div class="formula-box">Anthropogenic = Human-Induced + Environmental Impact</div><p><strong>Major Anthropogenic Activities:</strong></p><ul class="bullet-list"><li><strong>Deforestation — वनोन्मूलन:</strong> Clearing forests for agriculture, urbanisation, industries</li><li><strong>Industrialisation — औद्योगीकरण:</strong> Air, water, land pollution; resource depletion</li><li><strong>Urbanisation — नगरीकरण:</strong> Land use change, heat island effect, waste generation</li><li><strong>Agriculture — कृषि:</strong> Use of fertilisers, pesticides, irrigation, monoculture</li><li><strong>Fossil Fuel Burning — जीवाश्म ईंधन जलाना:</strong> CO₂ emissions, air pollution, climate change</li><li><strong>Mining — खनन:</strong> Land degradation, water pollution, loss of biodiversity</li><li><strong>Overfishing — अत्यधिक मछली पकड़ना:</strong> Marine ecosystem imbalance</li><li><strong>Waste Generation — अपशिष्ट उत्पादन:</strong> Plastic pollution, e-waste, landfill issues</li><li><strong>Transportation — परिवहन:</strong> Emissions, noise pollution, habitat fragmentation</li></ul></div>

      <div id="development-environment" class="topic-card"><div class="sec-head"><span class="sec-num">3</span><h2>Development & Environment — विकास एवं पर्यावरण</h2></div><p><strong>Development</strong> और <strong>Environment</strong> के बीच <strong>trade-off</strong> है — development के लिए resources चाहिए, जो environment से आते हैं।</p><div class="formula-box">Development = Economic Growth + Social Progress (but may impact environment)</div><p><strong>Key Issues:</strong></p><ul class="bullet-list"><li><strong>Resource Depletion — संसाधनों की कमी:</strong> Natural resources are finite</li><li><strong>Environmental Degradation — पर्यावरणीय क्षरण:</strong> Pollution, deforestation, biodiversity loss</li><li><strong>Climate Change — जलवायु परिवर्तन:</strong> Greenhouse gas emissions from development activities</li><li><strong>Displacement — विस्थापन:</strong> Development projects often displace communities</li></ul><p><strong>Approaches:</strong></p><ul class="bullet-list"><li><strong>Economic Growth vs Environmental Protection:</strong> Need balance</li><li><strong>Sustainable Development:</strong> Meeting present needs without compromising future</li></ul></div>

      <div id="sustainable-development" class="topic-card"><div class="sec-head"><span class="sec-num">4</span><h2>Sustainable Development — सतत विकास</h2></div><p><strong>Sustainable Development</strong> वह development है जो <strong>present</strong> generation की needs को <strong>future generations</strong> की ability से समझौता किए बिना पूरा करता है।</p><div class="formula-box">Sustainable Development = Present Needs + Future Generations + Balance</div><p><strong>Brundtland Report (1987):</strong></p><ul class="bullet-list"><li>First defined sustainable development</li><li>Report of the World Commission on Environment and Development (WCED)</li><li>Also known as "Our Common Future"</li></ul><p><strong>Three Pillars of Sustainable Development:</strong></p><ul class="bullet-list"><li><strong>Economic — आर्थिक:</strong> Growth, efficiency, innovation</li><li><strong>Social — सामाजिक:</strong> Equity, inclusion, participation</li><li><strong>Environmental — पर्यावरणीय:</strong> Conservation, resource management</li></ul></div>

      <div id="sdg-goals" class="topic-card"><div class="sec-head"><span class="sec-num">5</span><h2>Sustainable Development Goals (SDGs) — SDG</h2></div><p><strong>Sustainable Development Goals (SDGs)</strong> 17 global goals हैं — adopted by UN in <strong>2015</strong> — to be achieved by <strong>2030</strong>.</p><ul class="bullet-list"><li><strong>SDG 1:</strong> No Poverty</li><li><strong>SDG 2:</strong> Zero Hunger</li><li><strong>SDG 3:</strong> Good Health and Well-being</li><li><strong>SDG 4:</strong> Quality Education</li><li><strong>SDG 5:</strong> Gender Equality</li><li><strong>SDG 6:</strong> Clean Water and Sanitation</li><li><strong>SDG 7:</strong> Affordable and Clean Energy</li><li><strong>SDG 8:</strong> Decent Work and Economic Growth</li><li><strong>SDG 9:</strong> Industry, Innovation and Infrastructure</li><li><strong>SDG 10:</strong> Reduced Inequality</li><li><strong>SDG 11:</strong> Sustainable Cities and Communities</li><li><strong>SDG 12:</strong> Responsible Consumption and Production</li><li><strong>SDG 13:</strong> Climate Action</li><li><strong>SDG 14:</strong> Life Below Water</li><li><strong>SDG 15:</strong> Life on Land</li><li><strong>SDG 16:</strong> Peace, Justice and Strong Institutions</li><li><strong>SDG 17:</strong> Partnerships for the Goals</li></ul></div>

      <div id="intergenerational-equity" class="topic-card"><div class="sec-head"><span class="sec-num">6</span><h2>Intergenerational Equity — अंतर-पीढ़ी न्याय</h2></div><p><strong>Intergenerational Equity</strong> का अर्थ है <strong>present generation</strong> को environment और resources का उपयोग इस प्रकार करना चाहिए कि <strong>future generations</strong> भी अपनी needs पूरी कर सकें।</p><div class="formula-box">Intergenerational Equity = Fairness between Present and Future Generations</div><p><strong>Key Points:</strong></p><ul class="bullet-list"><li>Resources should be <strong>sustainable</strong> — not exhausted</li><li>Environment should be <strong>protected</strong> for future</li><li>Future generations should have <strong>same opportunities</strong></li><li>Core principle of <strong>Sustainable Development</strong></li></ul></div>

      <div id="intragenerational-equity" class="topic-card"><div class="sec-head"><span class="sec-num">7</span><h2>Intragenerational Equity — अंतर-पीढ़ी न्याय (वर्तमान)</h2></div><p><strong>Intragenerational Equity</strong> का अर्थ है <strong>current generation</strong> के भीतर <strong>fairness and justice</strong> — resources और benefits का equitable distribution.</p><div class="formula-box">Intragenerational Equity = Fairness within the Present Generation</div><p><strong>Key Points:</strong></p><ul class="bullet-list"><li>Resources should be <strong>equitably shared</strong> among all people</li><li>Developing and developed countries should have <strong>fair responsibility</strong></li><li>Marginalised and vulnerable groups should be <strong>protected</strong></li><li>Climate justice is part of intragenerational equity</li></ul></div>

      <div id="carrying-capacity" class="topic-card"><div class="sec-head"><span class="sec-num">8</span><h2>Carrying Capacity — वहन क्षमता</h2></div><p><strong>Carrying Capacity</strong> किसी environment की <strong>maximum population size</strong> है जिसे वह <strong>sustain</strong> कर सकता है — बिना degradation के।</p><div class="formula-box">Carrying Capacity = Maximum Population + Resources + Sustainability</div><p><strong>Key Points:</strong></p><ul class="bullet-list"><li>Determined by <strong>resources</strong> (food, water, space)</li><li>Exceeding carrying capacity leads to <strong>degradation</strong></li><li>Human activities can <strong>increase or decrease</strong> carrying capacity</li><li>Population growth must respect carrying capacity</li></ul></div>

      <div id="polluter-pays-principle" class="topic-card"><div class="sec-head"><span class="sec-num">9</span><h2>Polluter Pays Principle — प्रदूषक भुगतान सिद्धांत</h2></div><p><strong>Polluter Pays Principle (PPP)</strong> के अनुसार जो <strong>pollutes</strong> (प्रदूषण फैलाता है) उसे <strong>cost</strong> (cleanup, compensation, damage) भी वहन करनी चाहिए।</p><div class="formula-box">PPP = Polluter + Pays + Cost</div><p><strong>Key Points:</strong></p><ul class="bullet-list"><li><strong>Polluter</strong> should bear the <strong>cost</strong> of pollution</li><li>Encourages <strong>prevention</strong> and <strong>cleaner technologies</strong></li><li>Internalises <strong>external costs</strong></li><li>Recognised in <strong>environmental law</strong> (national and international)</li></ul></div>

      <div id="precautionary-principle" class="topic-card"><div class="sec-head"><span class="sec-num">10</span><h2>Precautionary Principle — एहतियाती सिद्धांत</h2></div><p><strong>Precautionary Principle</strong> के अनुसार यदि किसी activity से <strong>environmental harm</strong> का <strong>risk</strong> है, तो scientific certainty न होने पर भी <strong>preventive measures</strong> लिए जाने चाहिए।</p><div class="formula-box">Precautionary Principle = Risk + Uncertainty + Prevention</div><p><strong>Key Points:</strong></p><ul class="bullet-list"><li>Lack of scientific certainty is <strong>not an excuse</strong> for inaction</li><li>Preventive action should be taken <strong>before</strong> damage occurs</li><li>Burden of proof lies with the <strong>proponent</strong> (activity proposer)</li><li>Widely used in <strong>environmental policy</strong> and <strong>climate change</strong></li></ul></div>

      <div id="environmental-principles" class="topic-card"><div class="sec-head"><span class="sec-num">11</span><h2>Environmental Principles — पर्यावरणीय सिद्धांत</h2></div><ul class="bullet-list"><li><strong>Polluter Pays Principle:</strong> Polluter bears the cost</li><li><strong>Precautionary Principle:</strong> Prevention despite scientific uncertainty</li><li><strong>Intergenerational Equity:</strong> Fairness to future generations</li><li><strong>Intragenerational Equity:</strong> Fairness within current generation</li><li><strong>Common But Differentiated Responsibilities (CBDR):</strong> Developed countries should take more responsibility</li><li><strong>Public Trust Doctrine:</strong> Natural resources are held in trust by the state for the public</li><li><strong>Principle of Sustainable Development:</strong> Balance economy, society, environment</li></ul></div>

      <div id="environmental-impact" class="topic-card"><div class="sec-head"><span class="sec-num">12</span><h2>Environmental Impact Assessment (EIA) — पर्यावरणीय प्रभाव आकलन</h2></div><p><strong>EIA</strong> एक process है जो किसी proposed project/activity के <strong>environmental consequences</strong> का assessment करता है — before approval.</p><div class="formula-box">EIA = Project + Impact Assessment + Mitigation + Decision</div><p><strong>Steps:</strong></p><ul class="bullet-list"><li><strong>Screening:</strong> Whether EIA is required</li><li><strong>Scoping:</strong> Which impacts to study</li><li><strong>Impact Assessment:</strong> Identifying and predicting impacts</li><li><strong>Mitigation:</strong> Measures to reduce negative impacts</li><li><strong>Reporting:</strong> EIA report/document</li><li><strong>Review:</strong> Independent review of EIA</li><li><strong>Decision:</strong> Approval or rejection</li></ul></div>

      <div id="exam-traps" class="topic-card"><div class="sec-head"><span class="sec-num">13</span><h2>Common Exam Traps — सामान्य परीक्षा जाल</h2></div><ul class="bullet-list"><li><strong>Trap 1:</strong> Sustainable development = only environmental protection → ❌ It includes economic and social dimensions too.</li><li><strong>Trap 2:</strong> Intergenerational = intragenerational → ❌ Intergenerational is between generations; intragenerational is within the same generation.</li><li><strong>Trap 3:</strong> Polluter Pays = polluter pays only for cleanup → ❌ Includes compensation, prevention, and restoration costs.</li><li><strong>Trap 4:</strong> Precautionary Principle = always stop all activities → ❌ It requires preventive action, not necessarily a ban.</li><li><strong>Trap 5:</strong> Carrying capacity = fixed number → ❌ Can change with technology, resource management.</li><li><strong>Trap 6:</strong> SDGs adopted in 2000 → ❌ SDGs adopted in 2015 (MDGs were 2000).</li><li><strong>Trap 7:</strong> Brundtland Report = 1992 → ❌ Brundtland Report was 1987 (Rio Summit was 1992).</li></ul></div>

      <div id="practice-questions" class="topic-card"><div class="sec-head"><span class="sec-num">14</span><h2>Practice Questions — अभ्यास प्रश्न</h2></div>
      <div class="pyq-tag">Question 1</div>
      <p>Sustainable Development was first defined in which report?</p>
      <ul class="bullet-list"><li>A. Rio Summit 1992</li><li>B. Brundtland Report 1987</li><li>C. Stockholm Conference 1972</li><li>D. Kyoto Protocol 1997</li></ul>
      <div class="answer-box">Answer: B. Brundtland Report 1987</div>
      <p class="explain">Explanation: The Brundtland Report (Our Common Future) first defined sustainable development in 1987.</p>

      <div class="pyq-tag">Question 2</div>
      <p>Which principle states that future generations should have the same access to resources as the present generation?</p>
      <ul class="bullet-list"><li>A. Polluter Pays Principle</li><li>B. Precautionary Principle</li><li>C. Intergenerational Equity</li><li>D. Intragenerational Equity</li></ul>
      <div class="answer-box">Answer: C. Intergenerational Equity</div>
      <p class="explain">Explanation: Intergenerational equity is about fairness between present and future generations.</p>

      <div class="pyq-tag">Question 3</div>
      <p>The Polluter Pays Principle means:</p>
      <ul class="bullet-list"><li>A. Polluters should be exempt from costs</li><li>B. Polluters should bear the cost of pollution</li><li>C. Victims should pay for pollution</li><li>D. Government should pay for cleanup</li></ul>
      <div class="answer-box">Answer: B. Polluters should bear the cost of pollution</div>
      <p class="explain">Explanation: The Polluter Pays Principle states that those who pollute should bear the costs of pollution control and remediation.</p>

      <div class="pyq-tag">Question 4</div>
      <p>The Precautionary Principle is applied when:</p>
      <ul class="bullet-list"><li>A. There is no risk</li><li>B. There is scientific certainty about harm</li><li>C. There is risk but scientific uncertainty</li><li>D. Harm has already occurred</li></ul>
      <div class="answer-box">Answer: C. There is risk but scientific uncertainty</div>
      <p class="explain">Explanation: The Precautionary Principle applies when there is a risk of harm, even if scientific evidence is not yet complete.</p>

      <div class="pyq-tag">Question 5</div>
      <p>Which of the following is NOT one of the three pillars of sustainable development?</p>
      <ul class="bullet-list"><li>A. Economic</li><li>B. Social</li><li>C. Political</li><li>D. Environmental</li></ul>
      <div class="answer-box">Answer: C. Political</div>
      <p class="explain">Explanation: The three pillars of sustainable development are Economic, Social, and Environmental.</p>

      <div class="pyq-tag">Question 6</div>
      <p>The Sustainable Development Goals (SDGs) were adopted in:</p>
      <ul class="bullet-list"><li>A. 2000</li><li>B. 2010</li><li>C. 2015</li><li>D. 2020</li></ul>
      <div class="answer-box">Answer: C. 2015</div>
      <p class="explain">Explanation: The SDGs were adopted by the UN in 2015, with a target year of 2030.</p></div>

      <div id="one-page-revision" class="topic-card"><div class="sec-head"><span class="sec-num">15</span><h2>One-Page Revision — एक पेज में पुनरावृत्ति</h2></div>
      <div class="formula-box">Sustainable Development = Present Needs + Future Needs + Balance</div>
      <div class="formula-box">Three Pillars = Economic + Social + Environmental</div>

      <p><strong>Anthropogenic Activities:</strong></p>
      <ul class="bullet-list"><li>Deforestation, Industrialisation, Urbanisation, Agriculture, Fossil fuel burning, Mining, Overfishing, Waste generation</li></ul>

      <p><strong>Equity:</strong></p>
      <ul class="bullet-list"><li>Intergenerational: Fairness between generations</li>
      <li>Intragenerational: Fairness within current generation</li></ul>

      <p><strong>Environmental Principles:</strong></p>
      <ul class="bullet-list"><li>Polluter Pays (polluter bears cost)</li>
      <li>Precautionary (prevent despite uncertainty)</li>
      <li>Carrying Capacity (maximum sustainable population)</li>
      <li>CBDR (differentiated responsibilities)</li></ul>

      <p><strong>SDGs:</strong></p>
      <ul class="bullet-list"><li>17 goals, adopted 2015, target 2030</li></ul>

      <p><strong>Key Reports/Events:</strong></p>
      <ul class="bullet-list"><li>Brundtland Report (1987): Sustainable Development defined</li>
      <li>Rio Summit (1992): Agenda 21, UNFCCC, CBD</li>
      <li>SDGs (2015): 17 goals</li></ul>

      <p><strong>Exam Formula:</strong></p>
      <div class="formula-box">Sustainable Development = Environment + Development + Equity (Present & Future)</div>
      <p><em>UGC NET में Human–Environment Interaction & Sustainable Development के questions principles, equity, SDGs, and carrying capacity पर based होते हैं।</em></p></div>

